I shudder more looking at the actual nethack code than thinking about
any hypothetical reimplementation. (I do survive my system shock roll
in any case, though). Take a look at the monster movement code for
some examples of serious obfuscation.

Things such as dungeon layout are nominally scriptable, but if you
change it from the defaults, it's you who have to chase down the
resulting bugs (or try to figure out the bizarre patterns - not much
difference, since the layout scripts aren't documented anywhere AFAIK)

It's no coincidence that all the nethack spinoffs are much buggier
than nethack. It's not very maintainable or modifiable, it simply
doesn't scale well with practically all interactions hard-coded in C,
right down to the T-shirt messages.



